## Verdict

The shape of the work is right. Version dispatch and the terminal capability
closure are DERIVED from the pinned catalog rather than hand-listed, the
per-version closed structs make cross-version syntax refusal real, reader and
writer share one validation gate, and the package honestly declines to claim
migration it does not implement. Three things block acceptance.

## Blocking 1 — host-authentication bypass gate admits documented OpenSSH aliases

`internal/config/validation.go` `sshHostAuthenticationBypass`, reached from the
production entry `config.Load`.

Accepted today (probe through `config.Load`, all returned nil):

    ssh_args = ["-o", "StrictHostKeyChecking=off"]
    ssh_args = ["-oStrictHostKeyChecking=off"]
    ssh_args = ["-o", "UserKnownHostsFile=none"]
    ssh_args = ["-o", "GlobalKnownHostsFile=none"]

ssh_config(5) on this host documents `off` as an alias of `no` for
StrictHostKeyChecking, and `none` as the value that makes ssh ignore
user-specific known hosts files. §6.3 requires
`StrictHostKeyChecking=no`, an empty `UserKnownHostsFile`, "or an equivalent
host-authentication bypass" to fail configuration validation. The gate already
implements exactly this clause for the `no`, `""` and `/dev/null` spellings; it
misses the OpenSSH-documented synonyms for the same two settings.

`TestLoadRefusesEveryOpenSSHHostAuthenticationBypassSpelling` asserts nine
spellings and its name claims the vocabulary is complete. The claim does not
hold, and nothing in the suite derives the vocabulary from the OpenSSH value
grammar, so the omission is invisible.

Wanted: cover the documented value vocabulary for both settings (at minimum
`no`/`off` for StrictHostKeyChecking and ``/`none`/`/dev/null` for the two known-hosts
files), and pin each accepted-value class with a case, so a later narrowing of
the matcher reddens the suite.

## Blocking 2 — refusal clauses that survive individual disablement

The DoD requires each refusal clause to redden the suite when disabled
individually, or to be documented as provably subsumed with the subsuming check
named. Twelve delete-mutants and two narrowing mutants survive with the suite
fully green. Full killed/survived table in the evidence artifact. The ones that
matter most:

- `hasForbiddenConfigName` can be made to always return false and everything
  stays green. This is the §6.4 "no secret, endpoint credential, model token,
  auth root, or arbitrary environment passthrough" rule — a security refusal
  with zero negative coverage, at both the extension-key and nested-map sites.
- The printable/non-control rule and both `utf8.ValidString` checks can be
  deleted with the suite green; §6.3 states `host_name` is "1–64 printable
  non-control UTF-8 characters" and the character-bounds test only exercises
  length.
- The NUL-byte rejection on an `ssh_args` element can be deleted with the suite
  green. Endpoint and every SSH argument are passed as atomic argv values, so
  this one is worth a real case.
- Extension shape: the reverse-DNS namespace requirement, the key-length bound,
  the float refusal and the array-depth bound each delete green. The map-depth
  bound, the count bound and the byte bound are properly pinned — the array
  branch is the gap.
- Narrowing `values[index-1] >= value` to `> value` in both
  `validateSortedUniqueClosed` and `validateSortedUniqueDigests` survives: the
  sorted half is pinned, the unique half is not, for `required_capabilities`,
  `transport_policy` and `scan_root_authority_ids`.
- The 128-byte bounds on `terminal.backend_id` and on
  `external_trust[].backend_id` each delete green because the single fixture
  sets both to the same over-long value, so either one alone kills the case.
  If that subsumption is intentional, name it; otherwise split the fixture.

### Why the producer's sweep reported 0 survivors

`TASK-260830-17suox_clause-mutation-sweep-rework.log` reports "clauses found: 99
... total survivors: 0 / 99", and the task notes carry that forward as
"99/99 mutants reddened ... 0 survivors". That measurement is real but it is
measuring a narrower property than the DoD clause requirement, in two ways:

1. **Population.** Every one of the 99 entries is a `return configError(...)`
   statement in validation.go / schema.go / writer.go. The 19 bare
   `return ErrConfigValidation` sites inside `validateExtensions`,
   `validateExtensionValue`, `validateSortedUniqueDigests`,
   `validateSortedUniqueClosed` and `validatePrintableCharacters`
   (validation.go:735-836) are not in the population at all. That is where most
   of my survivors live.
2. **Granularity.** Neutralizing a return statement kills the mutant as soon as
   *any* test reaches that return. A composite guard such as
   `if len(key) < 3 || len(key) > 253 || !reverseDNSPattern.MatchString(key) ||
   hasForbiddenConfigName(key)` is one entry in the sweep and dies to a single
   fixture, while three of its four clauses remain unexercised. The DoD asks for
   each *clause* to be disabled individually, not each error return.

So "0 survivors" is not wrong, it is a proxy. Reported as satisfying the clause
gate, it is a completeness claim that does not reproduce: my per-clause sweep
over the same tree found 14 survivors. Whatever the fix, the sweep tool should
either enumerate clauses rather than returns, or its log and the notes should
state exactly which property it establishes.

## Blocking 3 — `external_trust[].enabled` is required but never read

`validateTerminal` registers `entry.BackendID` for every trust entry regardless
of `entry.Enabled`, so this is accepted:

    [terminal]
    backend_id = "com.example.term"
    [[terminal.external_trust]]
    backend_id = "com.example.term"
    executable_path = "/opt/example/terminal"
    executable_digest = "sha256:000...0"
    enabled = false

`Enabled` is decoded, presence-checked, cloned and re-emitted, but no production
path reads its value — a field that exists to gate and currently gates nothing.
§6.5 lists "untrusted executable" among the conditions that fail
configuration/activation. If the intended split is that `enabled=false` is only
an activation-time refusal and configuration may still hold the entry, that is a
defensible reading — but then say so in README alongside the existing
platform-lane-minimum note, and pin the decision with a test, rather than
leaving the field inert.

## Non-blocking, decide explicitly

- An explicit `transport_policy = []` is accepted. §6.5 calls it a "sorted
  unique subset ... default both"; an empty subset admits no transport at all.
  The text is genuinely ambiguous. Whichever way it goes, make it a decision
  with a test rather than a side effect of `validateSortedUniqueClosed`
  accepting an empty slice.
- `ownership.v0.5.0.json` binds `section:17.5` to `translateV2`. The only §17.5
  sentence naming Configuration behavior is the backup/atomic-migration and
  read-only-downgrade requirement, which README correctly says is out of scope
  here; the rest of §17.5 is Directory Node / Mesh RPC release compatibility this
  package does not touch. Consider dropping the 17.5 binding until the migration
  owner exists. §6.4 carries a milder version of the same shape and is more
  defensible, since the closed v2 registry genuinely lives here.

## Verified and accepted as-is

- `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...` and `go test ./... -count=1` all pass; all
  nine packages ok. Log: `.temp/TASK-260830-17suox/review-go-test.log`.
- Reader version set and terminal capability vocabulary are derived from the
  pinned catalog and fail closed when the pinned source grows a member.
- Per-version closed structs make cross-version syntax refusal real in both
  directions (v1 rejects `[directory]` and `terminal.backend_id`; v3 rejects
  legacy `terminal.backend`).
- Legacy translation maps exactly `tmux|conpty` and refuses other values before
  translation.
- Reader and writer share one `validateConfiguration`, so an unreadable value
  cannot be written.
- Errors render a static clause and do not echo rejected machine-local values.
- No file is written and no migration is performed; README states this rather
  than claiming migration support. No crash/idempotency evidence is required,
  because the delta mutates no durable state.
- The worktree tree OID was recomputed as `1234f3c9` before and after every
  probe; this review modified nothing.
